Z||000000||RobertVol||06-21-2001||10:33 PM||||About the best clutches....<P>I dont want to start a thing after the whole "FieroShop" post. <P>I had experience with the centerforce unit in my 88 3.8SC but sold it and don't think I need one like that for my stock 88 gt. <P>Maybe Centerforce II would be the way to go.<BR>but I want opinions and why you think its the best.<P>Any suggestions? I want the best holding clutch without alot of chatter.. <P>thanks in advance,,as always <P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/RobsGT2.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by RobertVol (edited 06-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||06-22-2001||12:06 AM||||I couldn't reccomend the Kevlar street clutch from the guys at West Coast Fiero.<BR>When it arrived,loose in a box with the motor mounts,having tumbled around on it's way across the continent to my garage,it turned out to be the wrong clutch set.<BR>Called up Chris,who insisted that my car had to be a four speed because of the trim package(S/E)or something,but eventually agreed to reimburse me for the replacement pressure plate and throwout bearing that were different if I were to ship him the odd parts.Sounded fair enough to me,so I did.<BR>I put three thousand miles on the car,nearly all of which were spent going from Detroit to Seattle,and then the clutch wouldn't dis-engage,without any event,or sound.<BR>A teardown revealed an exploded disc;hub parts,springs and pieces of material out under the diaphram/springs' pivot points.<BR>I gave Chris a call,and he said to send it back,and he'd see what broke.<BR>That was last Fall.<BR>The last thing I heard Chris say to me on the phone was that he'd get some $ to me;that was a couple months ago,and it was the same promise before that,and before that even.He also said during the second call,that the parts I substituted weren't good enough and that must've been why it failed.<BR>The stock replacement clutch has now seen over ten thousand miles with no problems yet.<BR>I don't even try and do burnouts or grab gears either.My engine idles at 1200 rpm and I was unable to get the car off the line without the horrible chattering that then quickly destroyed what was essentially a stock disc with oversized springs,and kevlar linings.<BR>Nice urethane motor mounts though.<BR>That's just been MY experience with that type of clutch,and with West Coast Fiero.<BR>||||reg||13
Z||000002||RobertVol||06-22-2001||12:53 AM||||Well,<P>That doesnt sound good.<P>Maybe I should look elsewhere <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>anyone else ? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||06-22-2001||01:13 AM||||In spite of what's been written about them, I've had good luck with the original equipment "LUK" (pun intended) in my '87 GT.<P>My original clutch went out at 116,000 miles from oiling through the rear cam seal, not from the disc lining or springs failing. <P>Horrors! I had a CHEVY DEALER replace it with another.  <P>I've been happy with that arrangement.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RobertVol||06-22-2001||01:48 PM||||I may go with a stock replacment if no one can justify the reason for spending money on a performance clutch. maybe there is no reason for using one in a stock 2.8 is there really?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000005||starfighter007@msn.com||06-22-2001||06:27 PM||||I think I have a kevlar clutch really not sure.last summer I swapped 4speed to 5 speed. looked at the clutch I could not tell if it was kevlar clutch disc. or not.and if it is kevlar I dont have any of the the chatering I ve heard people Tak about with these clutches.But if your driving habits are good where you dont need to do burn outs at every light  I think I would go with a stock cluth set and save a few bucks Just my thoughts. good luck||||reg||1
